# Optional read-only "server wordlists" library — lets the frontend offer a
# searchable dropdown of files next to each upload button, instead of only
# drag-and-drop / browser file picker. Feature is off unless both are set.
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in a real path to turn it on.
#
# Under `docker compose up`, NARWAL_WORDLISTS_DIR is a HOST path — it gets
# bind-mounted read-only into the container (see docker-compose.yml). With
# bare `uvicorn` (no Docker), it's read directly, so use a local path there.
NARWAL_WORDLISTS_DIR=

# Regex (Python re.search) a file's path — relative to NARWAL_WORDLISTS_DIR,
# forward slashes — must match to be listed. Empty matches everything under
# the directory. Example: mount_path=/usr/share/dict with the regex below
# surfaces files under /usr/share/dict/hashmob/cleaned/ and
# /usr/share/dict/rockyou/cleaned/.
#
# This is a real boundary, not just a UI filter: a mergeability job may only
# reference files that match it, so tightening the regex also narrows what
# jobs can run against.
# NARWAL_WORDLISTS_REGEX=(hashmob|rockyou)/cleaned/.*
NARWAL_WORDLISTS_REGEX=

NARWAL_RULES_DIR=

# ── hashcat worker ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Mergeability and Attack order scenes queue jobs that a hashcat worker
# container runs in the background. Both need NARWAL_WORDLISTS_DIR above to be
# set, since a job's goal list and attack wordlists have to be files the server
# can already see.

# REQUIRED under Docker. A HOST path to your own hashcat install — a release
# tarball, or a `git clone && make` build directory. The worker image does not
# ship a hashcat: running your own is what gives you the attack modes, hash
# modes and backends your distribution's package does not have, and a bundled
# fallback would let a broken mount quietly run the wrong binary instead of
# failing loudly.
#
# Bind-mounted at /opt/hashcat, read-WRITE. That is not an oversight: a hashcat
# built from source is a "portable" install, so it keeps its compiled-kernel
# cache and hashcat.dictstat2 next to the binary and will not start without
# somewhere to write (mounting it :ro dies with "Read-only file system", and
# XDG_CACHE_HOME does not redirect it). Keeping that cache on the host is also
# what stops every restart from recompiling kernels. The worker runs as root,
# so files it leaves in the tree are root-owned.
NARWAL_HASHCAT_DIR=

# Without Docker, point this at the binary directly instead.
# NARWAL_HASHCAT_BIN=/opt/hashcat/hashcat

# Where the job queue (narwal.db) and per-job hashcat artifacts are written.
# The one read-write directory the app uses. Defaults to ./runs at the repo
# root, bind-mounted into both containers.
# NARWAL_RUNS_HOST_DIR=./runs


# Pass --force to hashcat. Needed when the only OpenCL device is a PoCL CPU
# runtime, which hashcat refuses to use ("No devices found/left") without it —
# that is the default CPU-only setup, so compose turns this on. Set it empty
# when running against a real GPU, where those warnings are worth heeding.
# NARWAL_HASHCAT_FORCE=1

# Extra hashcat arguments appended to every run, shell-split. Operator config
# only — never reachable from the API. e.g. `-d 2` to pin a device.
# NARWAL_HASHCAT_EXTRA=

# Run one throwaway attack per attack mode before the measured ones. hashcat
# compiles an OpenCL kernel the first time it sees a given attack mode, and
# that one-time cost would otherwise land inside the measured duration of
# whichever attack used that mode first — which is the exact number an Attack
# order job schedules against. Measured on a PoCL CPU runtime: 29.5s cold
# against 3.3s warm for the same attack. Set to 0 to skip it.
# NARWAL_HASHCAT_WARMUP=1

# Seconds without a heartbeat before a running job is assumed abandoned and
# requeued for a clean re-run. Covers a killed or restarted worker.
# NARWAL_WORKER_STALE=120

# How long the idle worker blocks before waking to do housekeeping. The API
# nudges it through a FIFO the moment a job is enqueued, so this is a fallback
# and not the delay before a job starts. NARWAL_WORKER_POLL is only used on a
# filesystem that cannot host a FIFO, where the worker falls back to polling.
# NARWAL_WORKER_IDLE=30
# NARWAL_WORKER_POLL=2
